vkQuake: default vid_vsync to 2 on Phoenix (V3D 4.2), a three-image FIFO swapchain.

NEW for vkquake-drm (docs/gpu-new-lane/vkquake-perf.md, "Why f2 reads exactly 30.00 fps" and
"Result -- mig-vkq-g, mig-vkq-g-water, pace-vkq-g"). Mesa's display WSI offers FIFO only
(wsi_display_surface_get_present_modes), so upstream's default vid_vsync 0, which asks for
IMMEDIATE or MAILBOX, finds neither and presents FIFO anyway; on this stack vid_vsync only
chooses the image count (minImageCount 3 for vid_vsync >= 2, else 2) and whether present-wait2
caps the queue at vid_maxframelatency (vid_vsync > 0). With two images an image goes back to
the application only when the other image's flip completes, so vkAcquireNextImageKHR returns
at the vblank that shows the previous frame, the frame's single vkQueueSubmit starts there,
and a GPU frame of ~20 ms (perf-vkq-f2: 19.9 ms) flips on every second vblank: exactly
30.00 fps, acquire waiting 10.8 ms per frame.

Measured on the same spawn view, same binary (patches 0001-0009), only `+vid_vsync 2` added:
mig-vkq-g 29.71 fps (median, n=111), kmsbuf id=1,2, acquire ~10.8 ms, pwait 0 calls ->
pace-vkq-g 44.21 fps (median, n=113, max 44.48), kmsbuf id=1,2,3, acquire ~32 us, pwait
~1 call/frame at ~90 us (the latency cap is not the limiter), 0 compute jobs, same picture,
no tearing. vid_vsync 2 is chosen rather than raising minImageCount for vid_vsync 0 because
it is the configuration that was measured, it keeps upstream's meaning (the menu's "triple
buffer") and the runtime switch: vid_vsync is CVAR_ARCHIVE as upstream and its callback
(VID_VsyncChanged_f) recreates the swapchain, so `vid_vsync 0` or `1` still selects two
images at run time.

--- a/Quake/gl_vidsdl.c
+++ b/Quake/gl_vidsdl.c
@@ -109,7 +109,17 @@
 static cvar_t vid_width = {"vid_width", "1280", CVAR_ARCHIVE};		  // QuakeSpasm, was 640
 static cvar_t vid_height = {"vid_height", "720", CVAR_ARCHIVE};		  // QuakeSpasm, was 480
 static cvar_t vid_refreshrate = {"vid_refreshrate", "60", CVAR_ARCHIVE};
+#if defined(__phoenix__)
+/* Phoenix-RTOS: the Raspberry Pi 4's display WSI offers FIFO only, so every vid_vsync value
+ * presents under vsync and the value only picks the image count. With two images an acquire
+ * returns at the vblank that shows the previous frame and the GPU can start only then, so a
+ * ~20 ms GPU frame flips on every second vblank (30 fps). Default to 2 (the menu's "triple
+ * buffer"): three FIFO images, and present-wait2 caps the queue at vid_maxframelatency.
+ * vid_vsync 0 or 1 still selects two images (VID_VsyncChanged_f recreates the swapchain). */
+static cvar_t vid_vsync = {"vid_vsync", "2", CVAR_ARCHIVE};
+#else
 static cvar_t vid_vsync = {"vid_vsync", "0", CVAR_ARCHIVE};
+#endif
 static cvar_t vid_maxframelatency = {"vid_maxframelatency", "2", CVAR_ARCHIVE};		// max frames queued for display under vsync, 0 = uncapped
 static cvar_t vid_desktopfullscreen = {"vid_desktopfullscreen", "0", CVAR_ARCHIVE}; // QuakeSpasm
 static cvar_t vid_borderless = {"vid_borderless", "0", CVAR_ARCHIVE};				// QuakeSpasm
